---
title: Encryption model
description: How NeoWorks encrypts your data end to end, who holds which keys, and how recovery works.
---

Your data is encrypted on your device. The server stores ciphertext and public keys, and
checks permissions, but it cannot read your content.

## The account key

Every account has an **account master key**. It is created on your device when you sign up
and is never sent to the server in readable form. It is stored wrapped twice:

- by a key derived from your password, so you can sign in on a new device;
- by a **recovery phrase** shown once at signup.

Your password is never sent to the server either. Your browser derives a separate
authentication secret from it, and that is all the server sees.

The account key is unlocked only in the **account vault**, a page on its own origin, or on
the **authenticator** app, which holds it behind your biometrics. Apps never see it.

## Nodes and keys

Your data is a tree of encrypted **nodes**: each collection (calendar, contacts, photos,
files) has a root, with containers and items below it. Every node has its own key, wrapped
by its parent's key. Whoever holds a node's key can read that node and everything below it,
and nobody else can.

Every write is signed by its author, so a compromised server cannot forge content.

## Apps

When you authorize an app, the vault gives it keys for the nodes you pick, and nothing else.
The app runs the NeoWorks client library, which stores and syncs those nodes locally. Revoking
an app removes its access, and the affected keys are rotated.

## Recovery

If you lose your password, the recovery phrase restores your account. If you lose both, the
data cannot be recovered, unless you turned on **escrow**.

Escrow is optional and off by default. If you opt in, a copy of your account key sealed to
the escrow service is stored. Recovering through escrow always starts a waiting period, you
are notified, and you can cancel it.

## Audit

Every grant and revocation is recorded in a signed access log that your devices can verify.
